The old railway station had been closed for years before anyone thought of turning it into a library. The platforms were still there, cracked and overgrown, and the clock above the main hall had stopped at a quarter past four. When the town council finally agreed to the plan, volunteers spent an entire summer clearing out the rooms, repairing the windows and painting the walls a pale shade of green.

Today the building is rarely quiet. Children gather in the former waiting room for stories on Saturday mornings, while students spread their notes across the long tables that once held luggage and parcels. An elderly man comes in every afternoon to read the newspaper by the window, exactly where he used to wait for the evening train to the city.

Nobody has repaired the clock. The librarians say that visitors like it the way it is, a small reminder that the building once measured time in departures and arrivals, and now measures it in pages.
